Landscaper exit interview — sidebar-empty-rows (claude-opus-5)

Delegation: 6 explorers, 14 sowers, ~5 steps inline. 332 tests at branch
point, 416 at close.

DEVIATIONS
1. Built against the wrong hierarchy twice: persisted agent identity and
   delegation labels, which the operator's five-level model makes ephemeral.
   Also invented a rationale for his design ('subagent rows had been
   presence-only because...'), answered with 'thats never been the case'.
2. Scope grew from a rendering bug to transport validation, a harness hook,
   five charter rewrites and a new testing rule. Operator-directed
   throughout, but the growth itself was never put to him as a question.
3. Broke the operator's tmux panes twice: C-c to a mis-resolved target
   (also submitting a stray command as a prompt into the gardener's
   session), then pkill -f matching its own command line. Address panes by
   %id, never by index or process pattern.
4. Told a courier to dump a full environment to shared /tmp; blocked as
   credential materialisation, correctly. Names, not values.
5. Described the enforcement hook as a CANNOT before establishing it is
   bypassable and fails open. Corrected everywhere, after the fact.
6. Asserted twice without measuring: courier turn cost, and that
   permissions cannot be scoped per agent. Both wrong, both checkable.

WHAT HELD
Every real defect was found by looking at the running system, not by the
test count. Four tests on this branch encoded defects as contracts; the
suite was green against a sidebar rendering an empty pane. The operator's
static-fixture rule found a genuine bug within minutes of being stated.

7. Ran landscaper-teardown.sh from inside the worktree; its $wt path is
   relative to the MAIN checkout, so the .return-window marker appeared
   absent and I reported the spawn handoff as incomplete. The marker was
   correct and present (%0, default socket). Check your own invocation
   before concluding another agent's work is broken. Worth a follow-up: the
   script could resolve its repo root rather than assume the caller's cwd,
   since a landscaper closing itself is necessarily inside its worktree.
